---
/*
 * The site-wide draft notice (myne-md/myne-website#33).
 *
 * Myne is pre-alpha and its legal texts have had no legal review, and until
 * this component both facts lived only where a reader might never arrive: the
 * pre-alpha status in a footer badge, an FAQ answer on the landing page and a
 * block on /download/, the review gap nowhere at all. A reader reaching
 * /security/ or /spec/ from a search result met confident technical claims
 * about a system with no third-party audit (docs/threat-model.md section 6
 * records that absence), and /terms/ read as a published contract for a hosted
 * beta the public site does not advertise.
 *
 * It renders from LandingLayout, which every other layout bottoms out in
 * (LedgerFeedLayout and UserGuideShellLayout both wrap it, and
 * UserGuideArticleLayout wraps the shell), so one insertion point covers every
 * page that goes through a layout rather than a hand-picked subset that
 * drifts. No count is written here on purpose: the figure moves with every
 * page added, and `scripts/validate-draft-notice.mjs` is what actually holds
 * the roster. Routes that emit something other than a page (the OG images,
 * the feeds, robots.txt, llms.txt, the guide search index, the theme preview
 * iframe) use no layout and are out by construction, which is correct: a
 * notice inside the theme-preview iframe would be drawn as part of the theme.
 *
 * ── WHY THREE VARIANTS AND NOT ONE SENTENCE ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The same disclaimer is wrong on different pages, in opposite directions.
 *
 * `status` is the default and says the product is pre-alpha and that the
 * FEATURES described are not commitments. Two things it deliberately does not
 * say, both of which it said in an earlier draft.
 *
 * Not "no public release": /download/ serves v0.5.0 and downloads.myne.md
 * carries minisign-signed release hashes (the installers themselves are
 * unsigned), so claiming otherwise would be the same misstatement this notice
 * exists to avoid, pointing the other way.
 *
 * And not "nothing on this site is a binding commitment", which was the
 * wording until review. It renders on every page, so it landed over the
 * published SHA-256 list and the minisign key on /releases/ and /download/,
 * over /security/'s own "the protocol spec and threat model are the contract",
 * and by its own scope it reached back over /impressum/ and /privacy/, the two
 * documents this component carves out precisely because their content is not
 * disclaimable. threat-model.md section 3 makes this site the SECOND WITNESS
 * for release integrity, a different box and deploy chain "so a compromise of
 * one cannot make its lie agree with the other", and section 6 line 149 says
 * in as many words that "Each control is a real engineering commitment, not a
 * marketing line". A site-wide non-commitment sentence is the negation of the
 * control the threat model leans on, printed over the hash list it applies to.
 * The scope was never meant to be the site; it was meant to be the roadmap.
 *
 * `draft` goes on terms, nutzungsbedingungen, law-enforcement,
 * behoerdenanfragen, notice and meldung, and says one thing: the document has
 * not been through legal review and may change.
 *
 * It said more than that until review, and what came off is the point. The
 * earlier wording added "is not binding. It describes an intention, not an
 * agreement in force", which presupposes the document is an agreement. These
 * six are four different kinds of thing and only two of them are.
 *
 * notice and meldung are the published Art. 16 DSA notice-and-action
 * mechanism and the Art. 11 and 12 contact points; a regulatory duty is not
 * disclaimable, so the sentence bought nothing there and read as the provider
 * saying it does not regard its own designation as in force, on the page
 * impressum.astro routes readers to for exactly that procedure.
 *
 * law-enforcement and behoerdenanfragen are a factual inventory of what the
 * server never has (no email, no persisted IP addresses at any layer, no
 * connection logs, no readable content). That is threat-model A2 restated for
 * a different reader, rated Strong because "the server cannot disclose what it
 * does not possess", and it is not the kind of statement an intention-versus-
 * agreement distinction can describe at all. privacy.astro carries the same
 * list, so the site was asserting one inventory under two statuses.
 *
 * terms and nutzungsbedingungen ARE agreements, and there the withdrawal was
 * real rather than a category error, which is why it needed a decision rather
 * than a fix: a contract binds on acceptance, BetaTermsConsent.tsx opens
 * /terms/ as "the full text" behind the Accept button, and the clauses that
 * would have gone are the end-of-beta guarantee, the 90-day shutdown notice,
 * and the contractual basis for freezing an account for misuse, which is the
 * only remedy /notice/ and /meldung/ offer a substantiated Art. 16 notice.
 * terms.astro's own header records why that guarantee exists: "Skiff shut down
 * and its users discovered the terms had promised them nothing."
 *
 * `updating` goes on privacy and datenschutz and deliberately does NOT say
 * "not binding". app.example.com is live and accounts can be created, so GDPR
 * Art. 13 imposes an information duty that no disclaimer lifts. Announcing
 * that the privacy notice does not bind would not reduce the obligation, it
 * would only record that the controller does not stand behind its own notice.
 * An unreviewed notice is a correctable position; a disowned one is not.
 *
 * Three things it therefore will not do, each of which it did in an earlier
 * draft. It will not claim the storage commitments are "the ones the app is
 * built to": that reads as conformance to the whole `notStored` list, which
 * includes "IP addresses in any persisted form, connection logs, access
 * times", a reverse-proxy property across several origins at a layer no build
 * of the app can enforce. It will not hedge the notice's ACCURACY ("may not
 * yet describe every detail accurately"), because Art. 12(1) requires the
 * information in a precise and transparent form, so a controller publishing
 * that its mandatory disclosure may be inaccurate is an admission against
 * interest under Art. 5(1)(a) and 5(2) rather than a disclaimer. And its chip
 * does not read "Draft" / "Entwurf": that is the strongest signal on the strip
 * and it sat directly against the document's own "Effective 12 August 2026" /
 * "Gültig ab 12. August 2026", doing the disowning the sentence was written to
 * avoid. What is left qualifies the REVIEW STATUS and points at the effective
 * date, which is the promise the page already makes about its own versions.
 *
 * `none` goes on two pages, for two unrelated reasons, and only the first is
 * about what the notice SAYS.
 *
 * /impressum/. An Impressum is a statutory identity declaration under
 * section 5 DDG: it is complete or it is not, and "draft" is not one of its
 * states, so a notice qualifying its force would qualify a declaration whose
 * truth is not disclaimable. It is NOT that a notice would put the missing
 * address in writing: the page already states that itself, as a placeholder
 * reading "Anschrift folgt zum öffentlichen Start" (example-org/operations#9). The
 * page is unlinked, `noindex` and filtered out of the sitemap, which is the
 * mitigation that fits it.
 *
 * /themes/builder/. Purely mechanical, and the reason is written out in
 * `scripts/validate-draft-notice.mjs`'s EXCLUDED roster: it is the only page
 * in the site with body{overflow:hidden}, so the document never scrolls and
 * the strip cannot settle into its own flow height at the end of it. It would
 * paint over the bottom band of a drag-driven colour editor and swallow
 * clicks there, permanently.
 *
 * The variant is handled at the CALL SITE (LandingLayout renders no component
 * at all), so nothing here carries a branch for rendering nothing.
 *
 * The rule all four variants follow is recorded in the main repository as
 * design-notes/ops/0517: a notice here may qualify a document's REVIEW
 * STATUS, never its force and never its accuracy. That entry, not this
 * comment, is what a later editor rewording the variants has to read first.
 *
 * One question it leaves open, deliberately and on the record in
 * example-org/operations#26: /impressum/ delegates the Art. 11 and 12 DSA contact
 * points and the Art. 16 notice-and-action mechanism to /meldung/, and the
 * carve-out does not travel with the delegation, so those designations are
 * published under a review-status qualifier while the page pointing at them
 * is not. The four pages keep `draft` until that issue is answered, because
 * the surviving sentence claims only that the text has not been through legal
 * review, which is true of all four and is a statement about the text rather
 * than about the duty.
 *
 * `draft` and `updating` share the label ("Draft" / "Entwurf") by intent: what
 * differs between them is the claim in the sentence, not the chip. The label
 * is also deliberately independent of `data/release.ts`'s RELEASE_CHANNEL,
 * which is one string in one language. This one has a German twin, and a
 * channel that moves to beta does not by itself make an unreviewed document
 * reviewed.
 *
 * German pages pass lang="de". The whole site ships <html lang="en">, so this
 * is the notice's own language rather than the document's, and matching the
 * page's prose is what matters: a German legal text under an English
 * disclaimer invites the argument that the disclaimer was not addressed to
 * the reader of the text.
 *
 * ── WHY STICKY, AT THE BOTTOM ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Not dismissible, and not at the top. GlassNav is `position: fixed; top: 0`
 * on every route and the landing hero is `min-height: 100vh`, so a top strip
 * either overlaps the nav or pushes a full-viewport hero out of frame; a strip
 * in normal flow at the top disappears under the fixed nav entirely. The
 * bottom is the one edge no layout here competes for.
 *
 * `sticky` rather than `fixed`, and that is the second attempt. Fixed takes
 * the strip out of flow, so the page has to reserve room for it with a
 * body padding, and that padding is a constant guessing a height that depends
 * on how the copy wraps. Measured, no constant worked: at 320px the tallest
 * (German `updating`) notice is 111px against 104px reserved, and at 760px it
 * is 72.5px against 48px, so the strip covered the footer at both ends while
 * over-reserving 53px in between. Sticky keeps the strip in flow, occupying
 * its own height whatever that turns out to be, and still pins it to the
 * viewport bottom while the page scrolls. No constant, nothing to keep in
 * sync, and the print rule below reduces to one property.
 */
import type { NoticeLang, NoticeVariant } from "../data/notice";

interface Props {
  /** Which claim the notice makes. See the block above for why these differ.
   *  "none" never reaches here: LandingLayout renders nothing for it. */
  variant: Exclude<NoticeVariant, "none">;
  /** Language of the notice itself, matched to the page's prose. */
  lang: NoticeLang;
}

const { variant, lang } = Astro.props;

const COPY = {
  status: {
    en: {
      label: "Pre-alpha",
      text: "Myne is pre-alpha software under active development. Features described here are not commitments, and none of its security claims have been independently audited.",
    },
    de: {
      label: "Pre-Alpha",
      text: "Myne ist Pre-Alpha-Software in aktiver Entwicklung. Hier beschriebene Funktionen sind keine Zusagen, und keine der Sicherheitsaussagen wurde unabhängig geprüft.",
    },
  },
  draft: {
    en: {
      label: "Unreviewed",
      text: "This document has not been through legal review and may change.",
    },
    de: {
      label: "Ungeprüft",
      text: "Dieses Dokument wurde nicht juristisch geprüft und kann sich ändern.",
    },
  },
  updating: {
    en: {
      label: "Under revision",
      text: "This notice has not been through legal review and is still being revised. Changes are published with a new effective date.",
    },
    de: {
      label: "In Überarbeitung",
      text: "Dieser Hinweis wurde nicht juristisch geprüft und wird noch überarbeitet. Änderungen werden mit einem neuen Gültigkeitsdatum veröffentlicht.",
    },
  },
} as const;

const copy = COPY[variant][lang];
---

<aside class="draft-notice mono" role="note" lang={lang}>
  <span class="draft-notice-label up">{copy.label}</span>
  <span>{copy.text}</span>
</aside>

<style is:global>
  /* Mobile-first: the base block carries the phone values and the one
     breakpoint below raises them, so 760 is written once instead of as a
     759/760 pair in two directions that has to stay matched by hand.

     This block sits at template top level and NOT inside a conditional. Astro
     processes <style> only there; nested in a JSX expression it is emitted as
     literal text, which shipped a stylesheet whose first characters were the
     template-literal braces, so the whole sheet failed to parse and the notice
     rendered invisible while every grep for it passed. */
  /* What the strip covers, published so the two nested scrollports it
     overlaps can clear it.

     Conditioned on the strip being IN THE DOCUMENT, not on this block having
     shipped, and the difference is not theoretical: Astro decides which chunk
     an is:global block lands in, and today it lands in one that /impressum/
     and /themes/builder/ also link, so a plain `:root` would have reserved
     space on the two pages that deliberately render no notice while the
     comment here said it would not. `:root:has(.draft-notice)` is true
     exactly when there is a strip to clear, whatever the bundler does with
     the rule, and every consumer's `var(--draft-notice-reserve, 0px)` then
     reserves nothing on those two pages without needing a fallback constant
     of its own.

     A ceiling rather than the height. Every consumer spends it inside a
     scroll container or on scroll-padding, where over-reserving is invisible
     and under-reserving hides a control, so the two directions are not
     symmetric and the number is deliberately generous. The values are the
     component's own measurements one paragraph down (111px at 320px, 72.5px
     at 760px) rounded up, and they are in px because the strip's font-size is
     a fixed 11px: 7rem, the first attempt, scales with the root font size
     while the thing it reserves for does not, so the two move independently
     and the reserve can go negative. */
  :root:has(.draft-notice) {
    --draft-notice-reserve: 120px;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    :root:has(.draft-notice) {
      --draft-notice-reserve: 80px;
    }
  }

  .draft-notice {
    position: sticky;
    bottom: 0;
    /* Above the fixed nav (80) and the ledger/guide chrome (90), and
       deliberately BELOW the two full-screen overlays that claim 200: the
       guide lightbox (styles/userguide.css) and the theme builder's colour
       picker (pages/themes/builder.astro). A notice that covered an open
       lightbox would be the wrong outcome. */
    z-index: 100;
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    /* Literals, not tokens, and that is the second attempt too. The first
       version read var(--bg-2), var(--fg-2) and var(--accent) with these
       values as fallbacks, which is right on every route that never imports
       tokens.css and wrong on the largest subtree in the site. The user
       guide's shell imports styles/tokens.css, whose :root ramp is the LIGHT
       one, and scopes its dark values to `.ug-root` (styles/userguide.css).
       LandingLayout renders this strip after the slot, so it is a SIBLING of
       `.ug-root` and the lookup succeeded with the light ramp: --bg-2
       #f4f4f2 and --accent #2c4a7a, a near-white strip with a navy label at
       the bottom of every dark guide page. There is no palette to follow
       here anyway. LandingLayout pins html and body to #0a0a0a on every
       route, so the strip has exactly one ground to sit on, and the print
       block below is the one place it changes. */
    background: #0c0c0c;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(194, 163, 94, 0.4);
    color: #c4c4c0;
    /* 11px is --text-xs, the smallest step of the canonical scale in both
       tokens.css and the main repo's docs/design/colors_and_type.css. The
       first version went to 10px on phones to save a wrapped line, which is
       the wrong trade for a disclaimer: its only function is to be read, and
       for the `draft` form conspicuousness is what decides whether a clause
       withdrawing a document's force does anything at all. */
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .draft-notice {
      gap: 10px;
      padding: 11px 20px;
    }
  }

  .draft-notice-label {
    flex: none;
    /* Literal for the reason the block above gives: --accent resolves to the
       light ramp's iron-blue on guide routes. */
    color: #c2a35e;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* Printing a legal page is exactly when the draft status has to travel with
     it. Sticky already prints in place at the end of the document; only the
     dark palette has to go. */
  @media print {
    .draft-notice {
      position: static;
      background: transparent;
      border-top-color: #999;
      color: #000;
    }
    .draft-notice-label {
      color: #000;
    }
  }
</style>
